<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orne River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orne is a river in Normandy, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 170 kilometres long, which flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> at Ouistreham. It gave its name to the Orne <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. In antiquity it ran through the lands of Gallic peoples such as the Viducasses and Lexovii, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">D-Day</a> in 1944 British airborne troops seized the bridges near its mouth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orne-river.jpg" alt="Orne" width="330" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sebb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orne_in_Caen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Known from Pliny and later Roman sources, the Baiocasses lived along the Calvados coast west of the <b>Orne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/baiocasses">Baiocasses</a></li><li><blockquote>Their exact location is debated, but most scholars place them in the <b>Orne</b> region around Sées or Exmes.</blockquote><a href="/state/esuvii">Esuvii</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lexovii lived between the Seine estuary and the <b>Orne</b>, with a coastline on the Channel.</blockquote><a href="/state/lexovii">Lexovii</a></li></ul></section>
